  "full_subtasks": ["Simplify the quotient on its original domain", "Simplify the powered-fraction product on its original domain"],
  "derivation": "On the original-expression-defined domain, a: invert the nonzero divisor to obtain (-16/8)*x^((4n+7)-(3n+5))*y^(2-3)=-2x^(n+2)/y. b: the coefficient is 81^3*5^4/(25^3*27^4)=3^12*5^4/(5^6*3^12)=1/25, and the exponent of a is 3(4n-1)-4(3n-1)=1, so the product is a/25. These are equality identities on the original domains only, not a declaration that all values admitted by the simplified expressions are admitted by the originals.",
  "independent_law_check": [
    "For positive x or a the real-power rules give the stated identities for every n for which the original is defined.",
    "For negative x with both x^(4n+7) and x^(3n+5) real-defined under ordinary real rational-power evaluation, n must be rational with an odd reduced denominator: an even denominator cannot be removed from 3n+5. Then x^(n+2) is defined too; numerator-minus-denominator exponent arithmetic preserves magnitude and sign. There is no real-defined negative-base counterexample.",
    "For negative a, the real-defined denominator a^(3n-1) likewise forces a rational n with odd reduced denominator. Its fourth power is positive, while cubing a^(4n-1) retains the negative sign; the final exponent is 1 and the coefficient 1/25. Integer outer powers introduce no branch choice.",
    "If zero powers at zero are not admitted, zero-base cases requiring them are outside the original domain. If a convention does admit the zeroth power as the constant 1, the only possible x=0 nonzero-divisor case forces n=-5/3 and both original and simplified a-part values are zero; the only a=0 nonzero-denominator case forces n=1/3 and both original and simplified b-part values are zero. The boundary therefore does not depend on choosing either convention.",
    "Original part a requires y!=0. No cancellation authorizes evaluating the original at y=0. Neither result claims restoration of a zero original divisor or an undefined real power."
  ],
